<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00605</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>25a0b83e-fea7-4bb8-b928-ff14c92902a6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here are three types of situations. Answer the questions about each situation.</para>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00002">
      <title>
        <emphasis>Bank Accounts</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00001">A customer opens a checking account and a savings account at a bank. A maximum of
        $600 will be deposited into the two accounts, some in the checking account and some in the
        savings account. (They might not deposit all of it and keep some of the money as cash.)</quote>

      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para id="para-00005"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the amount deposited in the checking
            account and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> the amount deposited in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00008">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: 
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the dollar amount in a checking account and the y-axis represents the dollar amount in a savings account.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_01.svg"/>
              </media>
           </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00014">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00015"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. $500 is deposited in checking and $100 in savings. The total is
            $600, which is the maximum amount the customer plans to deposit.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">If the customer deposits $200 in their checking account, what can you say about the
            amount they deposit in their savings account?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00018">The customer deposits $400 or less in savings.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00019">For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00002">A customer opens a checking account and a savings account at a bank. The bank
        requires a minimum balance of $50 in the savings account. It does not matter how much money
        is kept in the checking account.</quote>

      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents</para>
          <para id="para-00022"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00024"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the balance in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->

      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00025">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00026"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values to the constraints of the
            problem (the checking account should never have less than $0), enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
            after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00029">
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the dollar amount in a checking account and the y-axis represents the dollar amount in a savings account.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_02.svg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00032">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00033"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The customer keeps $50 in savings and $50 in checking, which meets the
            minimum of $50 deposited in savings.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00034">If the customer deposits no money in the checking account but is able to maintain
            both accounts without penalty, what can you say about the amount deposited in the
            savings account?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00036">The customer deposits at least $50 in the savings account.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      </section>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00003">
      <title>
        <emphasis>Concert Tickets</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para id="para-00037">For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00003">Two kinds of tickets to an outdoor concert were sold: lawn tickets and seat tickets.
        Fewer than 400 tickets in total were sold.</quote>

      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00039">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para id="para-00040"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00041">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00042"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>400</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of lawn tickets and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
            represents the number of seat tickets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00043">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> after your equation (because you
            cannot have a negative number of tickets).</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer: 
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the number of lawn tickets and the y-axis represents the number of seat tickets.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_03.svg"/>
              </media>
           </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00047">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00048">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00049">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00050"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. 100 lawn tickets and 200 seat tickets were sold. The total is 300
            tickets, which is fewer than 400.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00051">If you know that exactly 100 lawn tickets were sold, what can you say about the
            number of seat tickets?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00053">Fewer than 300 seat tickets were sold.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00054">For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00004">Lawn tickets cost $30 each and seat tickets cost $50 each. The organizers made at
        least $14,000 from ticket sales.</quote>

      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise id="exer-00013">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00056">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para id="para-00057"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00058">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>,
            where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of lawn tickets and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of
            seat tickets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00014">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00059">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> after your equation (because you
            cannot have a negative number of tickets).</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/121fc7d48b7024ae528b71bf02c757bb2abd5a17.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00062">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00063">
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the number of lawn tickets and the y-axis represents the number of seat tickets.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_04.svg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00015">
        <problem id="prob-00015">
          <para id="para-00064">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00015">
          <para id="para-00065">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00066">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00067"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>250</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. 100 lawn tickets and 250 seat tickets. The ticket sales would be
            $15,500, which is more than $14,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00016">
        <problem id="prob-00016">
          <para id="para-00068">If you know that exactly 200 seat tickets were sold, what can you say about the
            number of lawn tickets?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00016">
          <para id="para-00069">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00070">At least 134 lawn tickets must be sold to make at least $14,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      </section>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00004">
      <title>
        <emphasis>Advertising Packages</emphasis>
      </title>
      <para id="para-00071">For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00005">An advertising agency offers two packages for small businesses who need advertising
        services. A basic package includes only design services. A premium package includes design
        and promotion. The agency's goal is to sell at least 60 packages in total.</quote>

      <!--Q#1-->

      <exercise id="exer-00017">
        <problem id="prob-00017">
          <para id="para-00073">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para id="para-00074"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00017">
          <para id="para-00075">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00076"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>60</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of basic packages
            and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of premium packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00018">
        <problem id="prob-00018">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00077">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00078"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the x and y values to the constraints of the
            problem, enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> after your equation (because the business will
            not have a negative number of packages).</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/41e534468511ee366a55eaf635a19ab9ff1afd95.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00018">
          <para id="para-00080">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00081">
            
              <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the number of basic packages and the y-axis represents the number of premium packages.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_05.svg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00019">
        <problem id="prob-00019">
          <para id="para-00082">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00019">
          <para id="para-00083">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00084">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00085"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. 50 basic packages and 30 premium ones are sold. The total is 80, which
            is more than 60.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00020">
        <problem id="prob-00020">
          <para id="para-00086">If the agency sells exactly 45 basic packages, what can you say about the number of
            premium packages it needs to sell to meet its goal?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00020">
          <para id="para-00087">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00088">The agency needs to sell at least 15 premium packages to meet its goal.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00089">For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00006">The basic advertising package has a value of $1000 and the premium package has a
        value of $2500. The goal of the agency is to sell more than $60,000 worth of small-business
        advertising packages.</quote>

      <!--Q#5-->

      <exercise id="exer-00021">
        <problem id="prob-00021">
          <para id="para-00091">Write an inequality to describe the constraints. Specify what each variable
            represents.</para>
          <para id="para-00092"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00021">
          <para id="para-00093">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00094"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>60</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of basic packages
            and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represents the number of premium packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#-->
      <exercise id="exer-00022">
        <problem id="prob-00022">
          <!--QUESTION
          IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
          <para id="para-00095">Rewrite your inequality using slope-intercept form. Then, use the graphing tool or
            technology outside the course. Graph the inequality that represents this scenario using
            the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <para id="para-00096"><emphasis>Hint:</emphasis> To restrict the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> values to the constraints
            of the problem, enter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">{</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">}</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> after your equation.</para>
        <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/41e534468511ee366a55eaf635a19ab9ff1afd95.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00022">
          <para id="para-00098">Compare your answer: </para>
          
            <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis represents the number of basic packages and the y-axis represents the number of premium packages.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_03_06.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00023">
        <problem id="prob-00023">
          <para id="para-00099">Name one solution to the inequality and explain what it represents in that
            situation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00023">
          <para id="para-00100">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00101">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00102"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. 10 basic packages and 30 premium packages are sold. The total value is
            $85,000, which is more than $60,000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00024">
        <problem id="prob-00024">
          <para id="para-00103">If you know that exactly 10 premium packages were sold, what can you say about the
            number of basic packages the agency needs to sell to meet its goal?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00024">
          <para id="para-00104">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00105">The agency needs to sell more than 35 basic packages.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Video: Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables</title>
      <para id="para-00106">Watch the following video to learn more about solving inequalities in two variables.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://openstax.org/r/inequalities-two-variables"/>
      </media>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00007"><title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <para id="para-00107">This activity will require a partner and either graph paper or Desmos.</para>
      <!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00025">
        <problem id="prob-00025">
          <para id="para-00108">Without letting your partner see it, write an equation of a line so that both the
            <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts and the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts are each between -3 and 3. Graph your
            equation.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00025">
          <para id="para-00109">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00110"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00026">
        <problem id="prob-00026">
          <para id="para-00111">Still without letting your partner see it, rewrite your equation as an inequality.
            That is, write an inequality for which your equation is the related equation. In other
            words, your line should be the boundary between the solution region and non-solution
            region. Shade the solutions on your graph.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00026">
          <para id="para-00112">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00113"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mi>≥</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00027">
        <problem id="prob-00027">
          <para id="para-00114">Take turns stating coordinates of points. Your partner will tell you whether your
            guess is a solution to their inequality. After each partner has stated a point, each may
            guess what the other's inequality is. If neither guesses correctly, play continues. Use
            another coordinate system to keep track of your guesses.</para>
          <para id="para-00115">What was your process for determining your partner’s inequality?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00027">
          <para id="para-00116">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00117">I know the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is between -3 and 3, so I can guess a coordinate value of
            <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> between -3 and 3 and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
    </section>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00008">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00031">
          <problem id="prob-00031">
          <para id="para-00118"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_2_13_2_SC"/></para>
          </problem>
          </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>


    <section id="sect-00009">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00010">
        <title>Graphing Linear Inequalities in Two Variables to Solve Applications</title>
        <para id="para-00121">Many fields use linear inequalities to model a problem. While our examples may be
          about simple situations, they give us an opportunity to build our skills and to get a feel
          for how they might be used.</para>
        <para id="para-00122">For questions 1 – 3, use the following scenario to write, graph, and find solutions to
          an inequality:</para>
        <para id="para-00123">
          <emphasis>Example</emphasis>
       </para>
          <quote id="quote-00007">Hilaria works two part-time jobs in order to earn enough money to meet her
          obligations of at least $240 a week. Her job in food service pays $10 an hour and her
          tutoring job on campus pays $15 an hour. How many hours does Hilaria need to work at each
          job to earn at least $240? </quote>
        <para id="para-00125">1. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be the number of hours she works at the job in food service and let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
            be the number of hours she works tutoring. Write an inequality that would model this
            situation.</para>
        <para id="para-00126">
          <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
       </para>
        <para id="para-00127">We let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be the number of hours she works at the job in food service and let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be the number of hours she works tutoring. She earns $10 per hour at the job in food
          service and $15 an hour tutoring. At each job, the number of hours multiplied by the
          hourly wage will gives the amount earned at that job.</para>
        <para id="para-00128">
          
            <media alt="Mathematical expression: 10 times x plus 15 times y is greater than or equal to 240. Above the inequality, labels indicate the term representing 10 times x is the amount earned at the food service job, the addition sign represents plus, and the term representing 15 times y is the amount earned tutoring. The phrase &quot;is at least 240 dollars&quot; represents the label for greater than or equal to 240.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_05_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>
        <para id="para-00129">2. Graph the inequality.</para>
        <para id="para-00130">To graph the inequity, we put it in slope-intercept form.</para>
        <para id="para-00131"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>240</m:mn><m:mspace linebreak="newline"/><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>240</m:mn><m:mspace linebreak="newline"/><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mo>–</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mrow></m:math></para><media alt="A graph with a red line sloping down from (0, 16) to (24, 0) on a coordinate plane. The region above the line and within the first quadrant is shaded light red. The line is solid so it is included in the answer region.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_05_02.jpg" width="300px"/>
            </media>
          
   
        <para id="para-00132">3. Find three ordered pairs <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> that would be solutions to the inequality. Then,
            explain what that means for Hilaria.</para>
        <para id="para-00133">From the graph, we see that the ordered pairs <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>24</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> represent three of infinitely many solutions. Check the values in the inequality.</para>
        
          <media alt="insert alt text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_05_02.1.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
        <para id="para-00134">For Hilaria, it means that to earn at least $240, she can work 15 hours tutoring and
          10 hours at her fast-food job, earn all her money tutoring for 16 hours, or earn all her
          money while working 24 hours at the job in food service.</para>

        <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00011"><note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Graphing Linear Inequalities in Two Variables to Solve Applications</title>
        <para id="para-00135">For
          questions 1 – 3, use the following scenario to write, graph, and find solutions to an
          inequality:</para>
            <quote id="quote-00008">Elijah works two part-time jobs. One is at a grocery store that pays $10 an hour
          and the other is babysitting for $13 per hour. Between the two jobs, Elijah wants to earn
          at least $260 a week. How many hours does Elijah need to work at each job to earn at least
          $260? </quote>
          <!--Q#1-->
        <exercise id="exer-00028">
            <problem id="prob-00028">
              <para id="para-00137">Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be the number of hours he works at the grocery store and let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be
          the number of hours he works babysitting. Write an inequality that would model this
          situation.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00028">
              <para id="para-00139">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00138"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>260</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
          </exercise>
          <!--Interaction
          End -->
          <!--Q#2-->
        
            <exercise id="exer-00029">
            <problem id="prob-00029">
              <para id="para-00141">Graph the inequality.</para>
              <media alt="...">
                  <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
                </media>
              <para id="para-00142"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00029">
              <para id="para-00143">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00144">
                
                  <media alt="Graph of an inequalilty on the coordinate plane.">
                    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_15_05_03.jpg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>

          <!--Interaction
          End -->
          <!--Q#3-->
        <exercise id="exer-00030">
            <problem id="prob-00030">
              <para id="para-00145">Find three ordered pairs <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> that would be solutions to the inequality.
          Then, explain what that means for Elijah.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00030">
              <para id="para-00147">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00148">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
              <para id="para-00149"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. For each of these coordinate points,
          if Elijah worked <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> hours at the grocery store and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> hours babysitting, he would
          earn at least $260.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!--Interaction
          End -->
        </note></section>
      </section>
    </section>
